1 ATTACH

   Gives the terminal to another process of the job, and waits until it
   comes back, as DCL's ATTACH and a program's LIB$ATTACH do.

   Format

     ATTACH [process-name]

2 Parameter

 process-name

   The process to attach to, as SHOW SYSTEM names it.  With
   /IDENTIFICATION, none is given.

2 Description

   The process must be waiting for the terminal itself: a process that
   SPAWNed a subprocess and waits for it, or one that gave the terminal
   away by ATTACH.  A SPAWN/NOWAIT subprocess, the process typing
   ATTACH, and a process of another job are refused (%LIB-F-ATTREQREF);
   a process that isn't there is %SYSTEM-W-NONEXPR.

   The terminal comes back when another process ATTACHes to the waiting
   one, or when the process that has it logs out (it then goes to that
   process's parent).  DCL then says so (%DCL-S-RETURNED).  A SPAWN that
   waits is over, its subprocess still there, when the subprocess
   ATTACHes back to its parent.

2 Qualifiers
/IDENTIFICATION

      /IDENTIFICATION=pid

   Names the process by its process ID, in hexadecimal, as SHOW SYSTEM
   lists it.

2 Examples

   1.VAX> SPAWN
     $ ATTACH SYSTEM
     %DCL-S-RETURNED, control returned to process SYSTEM
     VAX> ATTACH SYSTEM_1
     %DCL-S-RETURNED, control returned to process SYSTEM_1
     $ LOGOUT

     Moves the terminal from a subprocess to the console's process and
     back, then logs the subprocess out.
